163	<!-- ============================================================ -->
164	<section>
165	<h2>1. Connection Ports — What They Really Are</h2>
166	
167	<p>Every hardware block on the canvas has <strong>connection ports</strong> — the small blue circles on its edges. In Python they are instances of <code>ConnectionPort</code>, which is a <strong>child <code>QGraphicsItem</code></strong> embedded inside the block.</p>
168	
169	<h3>What each port holds</h3>
170	<table>
171	  <tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Type</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
172	  <tr><td><code>port_id</code></td><td><code>str</code></td><td>Unique name within the block (e.g. <code>"CH1_IN"</code>)</td></tr>
173	  <tr><td><code>label</code></td><td><code>str</code></td><td>Short display label painted near the circle</td></tr>
174	  <tr><td><code>parent_block</code></td><td><code>BaseBlock</code></td><td>The block this port belongs to</td></tr>
175	  <tr><td><code>connected_to</code></td><td><code>Optional[ConnectionPort]</code></td><td><code>None</code> when free; points to the peer port when wired</td></tr>
176	</table>
177	
178	<h3>Position is fractional — not pixels</h3>
179	<p>When a subclass calls <code>add_port("CH1_IN", "CH1", 0.0, 0.15)</code>, the numbers <code>0.0</code> and <code>0.15</code> are <em>fractions</em> of the block's width and height:</p>
180	
181	<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/gui/blocks/base_block.py:189</span>
182	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">add_port</span>(self, port_id: str, label: str,
183	             x_frac: float, y_frac: float) -> <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>:
184	    port = <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>(self, port_id, label)
185	    <span class="cm"># setPos is RELATIVE to the parent block's (0,0) corner</span>
186	    port.<span class="fn">setPos</span>(self.BLOCK_WIDTH * x_frac,
187	                self.BLOCK_HEIGHT * y_frac)
188	    self._ports.<span class="fn">append</span>(port)
189	    <span class="kw">return</span> port</code></pre>
190	
191	<p>So <code>x_frac=0.0</code> → left edge, <code>x_frac=1.0</code> → right edge, <code>y_frac=0.0</code> → top, <code>y_frac=1.0</code> → bottom. This makes port placement resize-friendly — if you change <code>BLOCK_WIDTH</code> the ports move with it.</p>
192	
193	<h3>Colour signals connection state</h3>
194	<div class="diagram">
195	  <span class="port-tag">● Blue = idle (free)</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;
196	  <span class="port-tag connected">● Green = connected</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;
197	  <span class="port-tag" style="background:rgba(251,191,36,.12);border-color:rgba(251,191,36,.4);color:var(--gold)">● Gold = hover target</span>
198	</div>
199	
200	<pre><code><span class="cm"># ConnectionPort._update_color()  — base_block.py:41</span>
201	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_update_color</span>(self) -> None:
202	    color = self.COLOR_CONNECTED <span class="kw">if</span> self.connected_to <span class="kw">else</span> self.COLOR_IDLE
203	    self.<span class="fn">setBrush</span>(<span class="cls">QBrush</span>(color))   <span class="cm"># fills the circle</span>
204	    self.<span class="fn">setPen</span>(<span class="cls">QPen</span>(<span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">30</span>,<span class="num">30</span>,<span class="num">30</span>), <span class="num">1.5</span>))  <span class="cm"># dark outline</span></code></pre>
205	
206	<div class="callout tip">
207	  <div class="callout-label">💡 Key invariant</div>
208	  If <code>portA.connected_to is portB</code>, then <code>portB.connected_to is portA</code>. The link is always symmetric — <code>connect_to()</code> sets both sides at once.
209	</div>
210	</section>
211	
212	<!-- ============================================================ -->
213	<section>
214	<h2>2. Wires — How the Connection Is Stored and Drawn</h2>
215	
216	<p>A wire is a <code>WireItem</code> — a <code>QGraphicsLineItem</code> that lives in the scene, separate from both blocks. It holds references to its two endpoint ports and redraws itself on every mouse move.</p>
217	
218	<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/gui/canvas.py:46</span>
219	<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">WireItem</span>(<span class="cls">QGraphicsLineItem</span>):
220	    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, src: <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>, dst: <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>) -> None:
221	        <span class="fn">super</span>().__init__()
222	        self.src_port = src      <span class="cm"># reference to source port</span>
223	        self.dst_port = dst      <span class="cm"># reference to destination port</span>
224	        self.wire_id  = str(<span class="fn">uuid.uuid4</span>())[:<span class="num">8</span>]
225	        pen = <span class="cls">QPen</span>(<span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">255</span>,<span class="num">200</span>,<span class="num">50</span>), <span class="num">2</span>, ...)  <span class="cm"># gold, 2 px wide</span>
226	        self.<span class="fn">setZValue</span>(-<span class="num">1</span>)       <span class="cm"># render BELOW blocks so ports stay visible</span>
227	        self.<span class="fn">update_position</span>()   <span class="cm"># draw the initial line</span>
228	
229	    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">update_position</span>(self) -> None:
230	        src_pos = self.src_port.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>()   <span class="cm"># world-space coords</span>
231	        dst_pos = self.dst_port.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>()
232	        self.<span class="fn">setLine</span>(<span class="cls">QLineF</span>(src_pos, dst_pos))</code></pre>
233	
234	<p><strong>The wire's ZValue is <code>-1</code></strong> — it renders below all blocks. That's intentional: you should always be able to see and click the port dots even when two blocks are close together.</p>
235	
236	<h3>What happens when you connect two ports</h3>
237	
238	<pre><code><span class="cm"># CanvasScene.connect_ports()  — canvas.py:185</span>
239	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">connect_ports</span>(self, src: <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>,
240	                   dst: <span class="cls">ConnectionPort</span>) -> <span class="cls">Optional</span>[<span class="cls">WireItem</span>]:
241	    <span class="cm"># Guard 1: no self-loops</span>
242	    <span class="kw">if</span> src.parent_block <span class="kw">is</span> dst.parent_block:
243	        <span class="kw">return None</span>
244	    <span class="cm"># Guard 2: no overwriting existing connections</span>
245	    <span class="kw">if</span> src.connected_to <span class="kw">or</span> dst.connected_to:
246	        <span class="kw">return None</span>
247	
248	    wire = <span class="cls">WireItem</span>(src, dst)
249	    self.<span class="fn">addItem</span>(wire)                          <span class="cm"># add to scene</span>
250	    self._wires[wire.wire_id] = wire            <span class="cm"># register in bookkeeping dict</span>
251	    src.<span class="fn">connect_to</span>(dst)                         <span class="cm"># sets both port.connected_to</span>
252	    self.wire_added.<span class="fn">emit</span>(...)</code></pre>
253	
254	<h3>What happens when you delete a block</h3>
255	<p>The scene must remove all wires attached to that block first, then unregister the block:</p>
256	
257	<pre><code><span class="cm"># CanvasScene.remove_block()  — canvas.py:143</span>
258	wires_to_remove = [
259	    w <span class="kw">for</span> w <span class="kw">in</span> self._wires.<span class="fn">values</span>()
260	    <span class="kw">if</span> (w.src_port.parent_block <span class="kw">is</span> block <span class="kw">or</span>
261	        w.dst_port.parent_block <span class="kw">is</span> block)
262	]
263	<span class="kw">for</span> wire <span class="kw">in</span> wires_to_remove:
264	    self.<span class="fn">_remove_wire</span>(wire)    <span class="cm"># calls port.disconnect() on both ends</span></code></pre>
265	
266	<div class="callout warn">
267	  <div class="callout-label">⚠ The disconnect contract</div>
268	  <code>port.disconnect()</code> sets <code>self.connected_to = None</code> <em>and</em> sets the peer's <code>connected_to = None</code>. If you delete the WireItem from the scene without calling <code>disconnect()</code>, the two ports still think they're connected — a ghost state that breaks future connection attempts.
269	</div>
270	</section>
271	
272	<!-- ============================================================ -->
273	<section>
274	<h2>3. In This Project — Reading Real Port Definitions</h2>
275	
276	<p>Every block subclass overrides <code>_setup_ports()</code> to declare its port layout. Here are two contrasting examples:</p>
277	
278	<h3>Oscilloscope — input ports on the left, trigger on the right</h3>
279	<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/gui/blocks/oscilloscope_block.py:41</span>
280	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_setup_ports</span>(self) -> None:
281	    <span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> range(<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">5</span>):
282	        <span class="cm"># x=0.0 → left edge; y stacks down: 0.15, 0.35, 0.55, 0.75</span>
283	        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(f<span class="str">"CH{i}_IN"</span>, f<span class="str">"CH{i}"</span>, <span class="num">0.0</span>, <span class="num">0.15</span> + <span class="num">0.2</span> * (i - <span class="num">1</span>))
284	    <span class="cm"># x=1.0 → right edge; y=0.5 → vertical middle</span>
285	    self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"TRIG_IN"</span>, <span class="str">"TRIG"</span>, <span class="num">1.0</span>, <span class="num">0.5</span>)</code></pre>
286	
287	<h3>MSP430 — GPIO on both sides, specials at top and bottom</h3>
288	<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/gui/blocks/msp430_block.py:29</span>
289	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_setup_ports</span>(self) -> None:
290	    <span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> range(<span class="num">8</span>):
291	        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(f<span class="str">"P1.{i}"</span>, f<span class="str">"P1.{i}"</span>, <span class="num">0.0</span>, <span class="num">0.2</span> + <span class="num">0.08</span> * i) <span class="cm"># left</span>
292	        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(f<span class="str">"P2.{i}"</span>, f<span class="str">"P2.{i}"</span>, <span class="num">1.0</span>, <span class="num">0.2</span> + <span class="num">0.08</span> * i) <span class="cm"># right</span>
293	    <span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> range(<span class="num">4</span>):
294	        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(f<span class="str">"ADC{i}"</span>, f<span class="str">"A{i}"</span>, <span class="num">0.15</span>+<span class="num">0.2</span>*i, <span class="num">1.0</span>) <span class="cm"># bottom</span>
295	    self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"VCC"</span>,  <span class="str">"VCC"</span>,  <span class="num">0.5</span>, <span class="num">0.0</span>) <span class="cm"># top</span>
296	    self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"GND"</span>,  <span class="str">"GND"</span>,  <span class="num">0.7</span>, <span class="num">0.0</span>) <span class="cm"># top</span>
297	    self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"UART_TX"</span>, <span class="str">"TX"</span>, <span class="num">0.0</span>, <span class="num">0.95</span>) <span class="cm"># near bottom-left</span></code></pre>
298	
299	<p>The pattern: <strong>inputs left</strong> (<code>x=0.0</code>), <strong>outputs right</strong> (<code>x=1.0</code>), <strong>power top</strong> (<code>y=0.0</code>), <strong>comms bottom</strong> (<code>y=1.0</code>). This is a convention, not enforced — the system accepts any (x, y) fraction.</p>
300	
301	<h3>How save/load captures connections</h3>
302	<pre><code><span class="cm"># canvas.py:to_dict() — what gets serialised</span>
303	<span class="str">"wires"</span>: [
304	    {
305	        <span class="str">"id"</span>:        wire.wire_id,
306	        <span class="str">"src_block"</span>: wire.src_port.parent_block.block_id,
307	        <span class="str">"src_port"</span>:  wire.src_port.port_id,     <span class="cm"># e.g. "CH1_OUT"</span>
308	        <span class="str">"dst_block"</span>: wire.dst_port.parent_block.block_id,
309	        <span class="str">"dst_port"</span>:  wire.dst_port.port_id,     <span class="cm"># e.g. "CH1_IN"</span>
310	    }
311	]</code></pre>
312	
313	<p>On load, <code>from_dict()</code> rebuilds a <code>port_map</code> keyed by <code>(block_id, port_id)</code>, then calls <code>connect_ports()</code> for each wire entry. If a block was silently skipped (e.g. the old "Function Gen" name mismatch) its port_id entries are missing from the map — that wire is simply dropped.</p>
314	</section>
315	
316	<!-- ============================================================ -->
317	<section>
318	<h2>4. hardware_docs/ — The Reference Layer</h2>
319	
320	<p>The <code>hardware_docs/</code> folder is where you put vendor documentation so it ships alongside the simulator. It has nothing to do with code generation automatically — it is a <strong>human-readable reference store</strong> that a future "New custom block from docs…" wizard will read.</p>
321	
322	<pre><code>hardware_docs/
323	├── keysight/     ← E36312A, 33522B, 34401A programmer's references
324	├── tektronix/    ← MSO54, TBS series
325	├── ti/           ← MSP430FR5994, C2000 series
326	├── nasa/         ← cFE, cFS, F′ Prime flight OS docs
327	├── rtos/         ← FreeRTOS, Zephyr, NuttX programmer's guides
328	└── custom/       ← in-house ASICs, development boards, anything else</code></pre>
329	
330	<h3>The suggested per-device layout</h3>
331	<pre><code>hardware_docs/keysight/E36312A/
332	├── programmer_reference.pdf   ← the full vendor PDF
333	├── scpi_commands.md           ← cleaned-up command table you'll test against
334	└── notes.md                   ← team-specific notes (quirks, tested versions)</code></pre>
335	
336	<h3>What Silmulator currently does with these files</h3>
337	<table>
338	  <tr><th>File type</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
339	  <tr><td>PDFs</td><td>Stored for human reference; not auto-parsed at runtime</td></tr>
340	  <tr><td><code>scpi_commands.md</code></td><td>The future custom-block wizard will read this to pre-fill command maps</td></tr>
341	  <tr><td><code>notes.md</code></td><td>Human reference only</td></tr>
342	</table>
343	
344	<div class="callout tip">
345	  <div class="callout-label">💡 The workflow loop</div>
346	  <ol style="margin-left:1.2rem;margin-top:.4rem">
347	    <li>Drop a vendor PDF in <code>hardware_docs/&lt;vendor&gt;/&lt;model&gt;/</code></li>
348	    <li>Write a <code>scpi_commands.md</code> summarising the commands you'll test</li>
349	    <li>Create a matching JSON spec in <code>silmulator/custom_blocks/</code></li>
350	    <li>Restart Silmulator — the block appears in the library automatically</li>
351	  </ol>
352	</div>
353	
354	<h3>Why a folder, not a wiki</h3>
355	<p>Lab benches, secure test ranges, and flight-test environments often have no internet. Keeping docs in the git repo means the Docker image and <code>.app</code> bundle always ship with the references your test code was written against — no "where's that PDF" hunt during a test campaign.</p>
356	</section>
