522	<h2>8. Scenarios — Check Your Understanding</h2>
523	
524	<div class="scenario">
525	  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario A: You want to simulate a custom relay board that has 4 output channels and responds to the commands <code>RELAY:CH1 ON</code>, <code>RELAY:CH1 OFF</code>, and <code>RELAY:CH1?</code> (returns "ON" or "OFF"). Describe the JSON spec you'd write — name the required fields and what you'd put in each.</div>
526	  <details>
527	    <summary>Show answer</summary>
528	    <div class="ans">
529	      <pre style="background:var(--code-bg);padding:.8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.82rem">{
530	  "name": "Relay Board",
531	  "manufacturer": "MyLab",
532	  "model": "RB-4CH",
533	  "color": "#5a3a1a",
534	  "ports": [
535	    {"id":"CH1","label":"CH1","x":1.0,"y":0.2},
536	    {"id":"CH2","label":"CH2","x":1.0,"y":0.4},
537	    {"id":"CH3","label":"CH3","x":1.0,"y":0.6},
538	    {"id":"CH4","label":"CH4","x":1.0,"y":0.8}
539	  ],
540	  "commands": {
541	    "RELAY:CH1":  "",       // write ON/OFF — write-only, no response
542	    "RELAY:CH1?": "ON",     // simplest stub: always returns "ON"
543	    "RELAY:CH2":  "",
544	    "RELAY:CH2?": "OFF",    // etc.
545	    ...
546	  }
547	}</pre>
548	      <p style="margin-top:.5rem">Note: the current custom-block system can't store state between commands (a real ON/OFF toggle), so <code>RELAY:CH1?</code> always returns the same literal. State-tracking requires a Python device class. The JSON spec is ideal for stateless instrument stubs.</p>
549	    </div>
550	  </details>
551	</div>
552	
553	<div class="scenario">
554	  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario B: You load a saved layout and notice that one block's wires are missing — they were there when you saved. Name two bugs that could cause this, and how each manifests in the code.</div>
555	  <details>
556	    <summary>Show answer</summary>
557	    <div class="ans">
558	      <p><strong>Bug 1 — device_type mismatch:</strong> The block's <code>device_type</code> string (set in <code>__init__</code>) doesn't match its key in <code>BLOCK_REGISTRY</code>. <code>to_dict()</code> stores the wrong string; <code>from_dict()</code> calls <code>add_block(wrong_name)</code> which returns <code>None</code>; the block is silently skipped; its ports never enter <code>port_map</code>; all wires referencing its ports are also skipped. (This was the Function Generator bug.)</p>
559	      <p><strong>Bug 2 — clear() not resetting registries:</strong> After <code>File → New</code> the old block/wire registries aren't cleared. On the next <code>from_dict()</code>, stale entries from the previous layout could interfere with the <code>port_map</code> build — older block_ids might shadow new ones with the same UUID prefix. (Fixed by the <code>CanvasScene.clear()</code> override.)</p>
560	    </div>
561	  </details>
562	</div>
563	
564	<div class="scenario">
565	  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario C: A colleague asks "Why do wires have ZValue=-1? Couldn't we just put them at ZValue=0 like everything else?" Give the technical reason.</div>
566	  <details>
567	    <summary>Show answer</summary>
568	    <div class="ans">
569	      Qt renders higher ZValue items on top. Blocks have ZValue=0 (the default). If wires were also at 0, the render order between a wire and a block would be undefined — Qt renders same-ZValue items in scene-item order (last added on top). A wire added after a block would render <em>above</em> it, potentially covering the port circles. Setting ZValue=-1 guarantees wires are always below all blocks so ports stay visible and clickable regardless of how many wires cross through a block's bounding rect.
570	    </div>
571	  </details>
572	</div>
