220	// ─── CHANNEL: ports ───────────────────────────────────────────
221	{id:'p1',ch:'ports',type:'w',
222	 q:'What are the four main instance attributes stored on a ConnectionPort? For each, give its Python type and what it means.',
223	 c:[{l:'port_id (str)',t:['port_id','str']},
224	    {l:'label (str)',t:['label']},
225	    {l:'parent_block (BaseBlock)',t:['parent_block','baseblock']},
226	    {l:'connected_to (Optional[ConnectionPort])',t:['connected_to','none','optional']}],
227	 m:`port_id (str) — unique name within the block, e.g. "CH1_IN"; used as the key in port_map during save/load.
228	label (str) — the short string painted near the circle on the canvas.
229	parent_block (BaseBlock) — the block this port belongs to; used in connect_ports() to reject self-loops.
230	connected_to (Optional[ConnectionPort]) — None when free; holds a reference to the peer port when connected. connect_to() sets both sides simultaneously.`
231	},
232	
233	{id:'p2',ch:'ports',type:'mc',
234	 q:'add_port("VCC","VCC",0.5,0.0) is called on a block with BLOCK_WIDTH=280 and BLOCK_HEIGHT=240. Where does the port appear in local block coordinates?',
235	 snip:`<span class="cm"># base_block.py:189</span>
236	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">add_port</span>(self, port_id, label, x_frac, y_frac):
237	    port.<span class="fn">setPos</span>(self.BLOCK_WIDTH * x_frac,
238	                self.BLOCK_HEIGHT * y_frac)`,
239	 opts:[
240	  {t:'x=140, y=0 — horizontal midpoint, top edge',c:true, w:'Correct. 280×0.5=140, 240×0.0=0. Top-centre is the conventional power-pin position.'},
241	  {t:'x=0, y=120 — left edge, vertical midpoint',c:false,w:'x_frac=0.5 maps to 140, not 0. x=0 would require x_frac=0.0.'},
242	  {t:'x=140, y=240 — horizontal midpoint, bottom edge',c:false,w:'y_frac=0.0 maps to 0 (top), not 240 (bottom).'},
243	  {t:'x=280, y=120 — right edge, vertical midpoint',c:false,w:'x_frac=0.5 → 140, not 280. 280 would require x_frac=1.0.'},
244	 ]
245	},
246	
247	{id:'p3',ch:'ports',type:'w',
248	 q:'Why does ConnectionPort.__init__ call super().__init__(-r, -r, 2*r, 2*r, parent) with negative x and y? What does that mean visually?',
249	 c:[{l:'centre the circle on setPos point',t:['center','centre','centred','centered','origin','middle']},
250	    {l:'-r,-r shifts bounding rect so pos is centre',t:['-r','-6','negative','shift','offset']},
251	    {l:'QGraphicsEllipseItem rect is relative to local origin',t:['local','relative','bounding']}],
252	 m:`QGraphicsEllipseItem takes a bounding rect in the item's own local coordinates, with (0,0) at the top-left. By using (-r, -r, 2r, 2r) the circle's bounding rect is centred on the item's local origin. When setPos(x, y) is called, that origin lands exactly at (x,y) in the parent's coordinate system. Without the offset, the top-left corner of the circle (not its centre) would sit at the port position — the dot would appear shifted half a radius down and right of the block edge.`
253	},
254	
255	{id:'p4',ch:'ports',type:'w',
256	 q:'The Oscilloscope block has BLOCK_WIDTH=300 and BLOCK_HEIGHT=220. It adds CH1_IN at x_frac=0.0, y_frac=0.15. What pixel position is that, and which edge of the block is it on?',
257	 snip:`<span class="cm"># oscilloscope_block.py:41</span>
258	<span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> range(<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">5</span>):
259	    self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(f<span class="str">"CH{i}_IN"</span>, f<span class="str">"CH{i}"</span>,
260	                  <span class="num">0.0</span>, <span class="num">0.15</span> + <span class="num">0.2</span> * (i - <span class="num">1</span>))`,
261	 c:[{l:'x=0 (left edge)',t:['x=0','left','0.0 ×','0 px']},
262	    {l:'y=33 (0.15 × 220)',t:['y=33','33','0.15 × 220','0.15×220']},
263	    {l:'left edge of block',t:['left edge','left side']}],
264	 m:`x = 300 × 0.0 = 0 px → the left edge of the block.
265	y = 220 × 0.15 = 33 px → 33 pixels from the top.
266	So CH1_IN sits at the left edge, about 15% down from the top. CH2_IN would be at y = 220 × 0.35 = 77 px, and so on (each 0.2 × 220 = 44 px lower).`
267	},
268	
269	// ─── CHANNEL: wires ───────────────────────────────────────────
270	{id:'w1',ch:'wires',type:'mc',
271	 q:'Why does WireItem call self.setZValue(-1) in __init__?',
272	 snip:`<span class="cm"># canvas.py:57</span>
273	self.<span class="fn">setZValue</span>(-<span class="num">1</span>)   <span class="cm"># render BELOW blocks</span>`,
274	 opts:[
275	  {t:'So the wire renders behind all blocks (ZValue 0), keeping port circles visible on top.',c:true, w:'Correct. Lower ZValue → painted first → underneath. Blocks default to 0, ports inherit the block\'s layer. Wires at -1 are always below both.'},
276	  {t:'So mouse events on the wire are suppressed, preventing accidental wire selection.',c:false,w:'ZValue controls render order, not hit-testing. Mouse-event suppression is done via setAcceptedMouseButtons/setFlag instead.'},
277	  {t:'So the wire appears dimmer than blocks, giving a visual hierarchy hint to users.',c:false,w:'ZValue is a depth/order property; it has no effect on colour, opacity, or brightness.'},
278	  {t:'So the wire is skipped during scene.items() iteration in _port_at().',c:false,w:'_port_at() skips the temp wire by identity check (item is self._temp_wire), not by ZValue.'},
279	 ]
280	},
281	
282	{id:'w2',ch:'wires',type:'w',
283	 q:'connect_ports(src, dst) has two guards before it creates a WireItem. What are they, and what happens when each fires?',
284	 snip:`<span class="cm"># canvas.py:185</span>
285	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">connect_ports</span>(self, src, dst):
286	    <span class="kw">if</span> src.parent_block <span class="kw">is</span> dst.parent_block:
287	        <span class="kw">return None</span>
288	    <span class="kw">if</span> src.connected_to <span class="kw">or</span> dst.connected_to:
289	        <span class="kw">return None</span>`,
290	 c:[{l:'self-loop guard: same parent_block → None',t:['same block','self-loop','parent_block','same parent']},
291	    {l:'already-connected guard: either port busy → None',t:['already','connected_to','busy','occupied']},
292	    {l:'both return None silently',t:['return none','none','silent','no exception','no wire']}],
293	 m:`Guard 1 — self-loop: if src.parent_block is dst.parent_block, the user tried to wire a block to itself. Returns None immediately; no wire or signal is emitted.
294	Guard 2 — overwrite protection: if either port already has a connected_to peer, wiring it again would orphan the existing connection without removing the old wire. Returns None; the existing connection is preserved.
295	Both failures are silent (no exception), so the UI simply stays unchanged.`
296	},
297	
298	{id:'w3',ch:'wires',type:'w',
299	 q:'Describe exactly what _remove_wire(wire) does, in order. What state does it clean up and why does the order matter?',
300	 snip:`<span class="cm"># canvas.py:203</span>
301	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_remove_wire</span>(self, wire):
302	    wire.src_port.<span class="fn">disconnect</span>()
303	    wire.dst_port.<span class="fn">disconnect</span>()
304	    self._wires.<span class="fn">pop</span>(wire.wire_id, <span class="kw">None</span>)
305	    self.<span class="fn">removeItem</span>(wire)
306	    self.wire_removed.<span class="fn">emit</span>(wire.wire_id)`,
307	 c:[{l:'port.disconnect() nulls both sides connected_to',t:['disconnect','null','none','connected_to','both side']},
308	    {l:'_wires dict is popped',t:['_wires','pop','dict','registry']},
309	    {l:'removeItem removes from scene',t:['removeitem','scene','remove']},
310	    {l:'wire_removed signal emitted',t:['signal','emit','wire_removed']}],
311	 m:`1. src_port.disconnect() — sets src.connected_to = None AND clears the peer's connected_to. Doing this before removeItem means the ports update their colour (back to blue) while the scene is still valid.
312	2. dst_port.disconnect() — symmetric; clears the other side. (In practice src.disconnect() already nulls both, but calling both is defensive.)
313	3. _wires.pop(wire.wire_id, None) — removes the WireItem from the scene's bookkeeping dict so it doesn't appear in to_dict() or future lookups.
314	4. removeItem(wire) — deletes the C++ QGraphicsLineItem from the scene.
315	5. wire_removed.emit — notifies listeners (e.g. status bar) that a wire is gone.
316	Order matters: if removeItem ran first, the C++ object would be invalid and calling disconnect() or accessing port references through the wire would crash.`
317	},
318	
319	{id:'w4',ch:'wires',type:'mc',
320	 q:'update_position() is called on every WireItem during mouseMoveEvent. What does it do and when is it needed?',
321	 snip:`<span class="cm"># canvas.py:60</span>
322	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">update_position</span>(self) -> None:
323	    src_pos = self.src_port.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>()
324	    dst_pos = self.dst_port.<span class="fn">scenePos</span>()
325	    self.<span class="fn">setLine</span>(<span class="cls">QLineF</span>(src_pos, dst_pos))`,
326	 opts:[
327	  {t:'Redraws the wire by querying current world-space port positions, keeping it taut when a block is dragged.',c:true, w:'Correct. scenePos() returns the port\'s current world coordinates. When a block moves, its ports move with it; the wire must re-query those coords to stay anchored.'},
328	  {t:'Animates the wire colour from gold to green to confirm a successful connection.',c:false,w:'update_position() only calls setLine(); it doesn\'t touch the pen or colour at all.'},
329	  {t:'Recalculates the ZValue so the wire stays under any blocks that overlap it.',c:false,w:'ZValue is set once in __init__ and never changes. update_position() only updates the line endpoints.'},
330	  {t:'Checks whether the two connected ports are still on the same block and deletes the wire if so.',c:false,w:'There\'s no validation in update_position(); it only reads scenePos() and calls setLine().'},
331	 ]
332	},
333	
334	{id:'w5',ch:'wires',type:'w',
335	 q:'If you delete a WireItem from the scene (removeItem) WITHOUT calling port.disconnect() first, what breaks? Be specific.',
336	 c:[{l:'connected_to still set — ghost state',t:['connected_to','ghost','still set','not null','not none']},
337	    {l:'ports still green / show connected',t:['green','colour','color','_update_color','visual']},
338	    {l:'connect_ports() will refuse future connections',t:['connect_ports','refuse','guard','already','overwrite']}],
339	 m:`The two ports (src and dst) still hold references to each other via connected_to. This "ghost" state has three consequences:
340	1. The port dots remain green (COLOR_CONNECTED) even though no wire is visible, confusing the user.
341	2. connect_ports() guard 2 ("if src.connected_to or dst.connected_to: return None") fires — both ports appear "busy" so any future connection attempt to either port is silently rejected.
342	3. to_dict() looks for wires via self._wires, so the wire won't be serialised — but the ports still think they're connected, creating an inconsistency between the layout file and runtime state.`
343	},
